WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.340
How does dynamic programming find

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.840
the minimum number of coins?

3
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.360
We will define an exact-amount

4
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.040
state, compare every possible final

5
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.000
coin, fill a small table

6
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.180
and reconstruct an optimal answer

7
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.320
that greedy misses.

8
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.820
Define the entry at amount

9
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.440
a as the minimum coin

10
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.520
count making that exact amount.

11
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.120
The empty amount needs zero

12
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.760
coins, while an unreachable positive

13
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.540
amount must have an infinity

14
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.360
marker rather than a misleading

15
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.420
count of zero.

16
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.520
In any nonempty solution, remove

17
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.160
its last coin of value

18
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.240
d. The remaining coins must

19
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.880
make amount a minus d,

20
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.600
so trying every affordable denomination

21
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.280
gives one plus the best

22
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.920
stored count for that remainder.

23
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.020
Why may we use an

24
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.600
optimal remainder solution? If that

25
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.240
remainder used more coins than

26
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.060
necessary, replacing it by a

27
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.760
better solution and restoring the

28
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.360
last coin would improve the

29
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.380
supposed optimum, a contradiction.

30
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.500
Use denominations one, three and

31
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.640
four, and target amount six.

32
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.340
Start from C of zero

33
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.900
equal to zero and fill

34
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.540
increasing amounts, because every positive

35
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.260
denomination points to a smaller

36
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:11.620
amount.

37
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.280
For amount one, only the

38
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.380
one-coin fits, so the answer

39
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.200
is one. For amount two,

40
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.720
that same denomination extends C

41
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.640
of one, giving two coins.

42
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.560
For amount three, compare three

43
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.280
ones with the single three-coin,

44
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.080
so the minimum is one.

45
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.000
For amount four, the single

46
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.780
four-coin beats both ways of

47
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.440
extending earlier amounts, so the

48
00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.320
minimum is again one.

49
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.000
Amount five has candidates two,

50
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.340
three and two, so its

51
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.580
optimum is two coins. At

52
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.320
amount six, the candidates are

53
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.880
three using a final one,

54
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.020
two using a final three,

55
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.620
and three using a final

56
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.260
four, so choose two.

57
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.200
To reconstruct six, select a

58
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.880
coin of value three and

59
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.520
reduce the remaining amount to

60
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.880
three. Select another three-coin, reach

61
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.140
zero, and recover two coins

62
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.000
of value three.

63
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.500
The largest-first rule would take

64
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.620
four and then two ones,

65
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.560
for three coins instead of

66
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.060
two. The dynamic program succeeds

67
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.100
because it compares alternatives rather

68
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.700
than permanently committing to the

69
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:19.820
largest denomination.

70
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.020
For a target A and

71
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.220
k denominations, there are A

72
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.980
positive amounts and at most

73
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.400
k candidates per amount. This

74
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.700
gives big O of k

75
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.400
A time and big O

76
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.100
of A table storage, with

77
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.500
reconstruction adding work proportional to

78
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.660
the number of coins output.

79
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.680
If coin one is absent,

80
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.880
some amounts may be unreachable;

81
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.840
with denominations three and four,

82
00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.040
amount two is one example.

83
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.460
Keep those entries at infinity

84
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.900
and report impossibility, rather than

85
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.460
following a nonexistent reconstruction choice.

86
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.420
Things to take note of:

87
00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.360
the state means exactly an

88
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.180
amount, and its objective is

89
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.020
minimum count, not the number

90
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.420
of combinations. Derive cost from

91
00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.720
actual states and choices, avoid

92
00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.960
the slide's unsupported blanket factorial

93
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.020
claim, and remember that dependence

94
00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.280
on the numeric amount will

95
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.080
matter when we discuss input

96
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:12.360
bits.
